@meta
id: 05-02
title: Coming back a word
chapter: 05
type: lesson
skills: word-b, word-w
par: 19
minutes: 3
vscode: Ctrl-Left
open: ticket.md

@brief
You will overshoot. Everyone overshoots — you press `w` one time too many
and land past the thing you were aiming at. The fix is not to undo the
motion in your head, it is to walk back.

This time do not travel forwards at all. Jump to the end of the line and
come back. The line should read:

    Refunds issued after the cutoff are still charged to the closed customer account.

@keys
| `b` | back to the start of the previous word |
| `w` | forward to the start of the next word |
| `$` | end of the line |
| `i` | insert to the left of the cursor |

@hints
`$` puts you on the last character of the line in one press. From there
the target is only a couple of words back.
--
`b` is `w` in reverse. It also lands on the first character of a word,
which is again where an insert wants to start.
--
`$` then `b` then `b` sits you on the `c` of `customer`. Then `i`, type
`closed` and a space, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    $ bb  i closed <Esc>  :wq<Enter>

`b` and `w` both stop on the first character of a word, so they are
mirror images and you can bounce between them freely. Overshoot by one,
press `b`, carry on. There is no penalty and no mode to get out of.

Watch what `b` did at the end of the line: it stepped over the full stop
as if it were a word of its own, because to vim it is one. Hold that
thought — the fifth lesson of this chapter is entirely about it, and in code it changes
everything.

Coming at a target from the right is a real technique, not a consolation
prize. When the thing you want is nearer the end of the line than the
start, `$` and two `b` presses beat nine `w` presses, and you did not
have to count anything.

@start ticket.md
Refunds issued after the cutoff are still charged to the customer account.

@expected ticket.md
Refunds issued after the cutoff are still charged to the closed customer account.
